<!--
  ==================================================================================================
  PowerFramework.slnx
  The repository root solution for the .NET 10 tree added to this checkout by the PowerFramework
  decomposition, Phase 1.
  ==================================================================================================

  WHAT THIS FILE IS, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY IS NOT

  This solution enumerates every buildable project in the refactor so that one developer, or one
  single pass whole estate build, can restore, build and test all of it from the repository root:

      dotnet restore PowerFramework.slnx
      dotnet build   PowerFramework.slnx -c Release
      dotnet test    PowerFramework.slnx

  That is the whole of its purpose. It is a CONVENIENCE VIEW and nothing more.

  It is emphatically NOT the mechanism that makes the four services independently buildable and
  independently deployable. That mechanism is the four per service solutions, one per service
  directory:

      services/gateway-service/gateway-service.slnx
      services/dataservices-service/dataservices-service.slnx
      services/persistence-service/persistence-service.slnx
      services/security-service/security-service.slnx

  Those four and this one coexist deliberately, and neither kind supersedes the other. Solution
  discovery inspects the CURRENT directory only and never walks up, so this file cannot be found by,
  and cannot interfere with, a bare command issued inside a service directory. A developer who has
  only ever built one service, from inside that service's own directory, succeeds without this file
  ever being read.

  Nothing may be arranged so that this file becomes a prerequisite for a single service build, and
  nothing is: the four per service solutions, the four container definitions and the per service CI
  jobs each name their own projects directly and never reference this one. A single collapsed
  solution is a failure mode for this refactor rather than a cautious default, which is why the
  convenience nature of this file is stated in the same breath as its purpose.

  FORMAT: .slnx, THE XML SOLUTION FORMAT. NEVER A .sln, AND NEVER A .slnf.

  .NET 10 tooling emits .slnx rather than the legacy text .sln format. Measured on the pinned SDK
  10.0.302 before this file was written: `dotnet new sln` produces a bare `Solution` root element,
  and adding a project produces a `Project` child carrying a `Path` attribute with forward slash
  separators, relative to this file's own directory. A solution folder is a `Folder` element
  wrapping those children, its `Name` carrying both a leading and a trailing slash. The body below
  follows that shape exactly, because it was generated with the SDK and then annotated rather than
  hand written.

  A legacy solution FILTER, the .slnf format, is not authored here and must not be - because a
  filter's project list has to be kept in exact agreement with the solution BY HAND, and every
  disagreement fails hard in all three verbs. An earlier form of this note said a filter over a
  .slnx fails with MSB4014; that was measured false on the pinned SDK, where a well formed filter
  over an existing .slnx restores, builds and tests cleanly. docs/BUILD.md section 2 carries the
  measurement, the four failing situations and the code's real trigger. The prohibition stands on
  the maintenance ground, which does not expire the way a tooling defect would.
  Do not author one, now or as a follow up. To scope a build to a subset of projects, use the per
  service solution for the service in question, which is precisely why those four exist. There is
  likewise no .sln file anywhere in this repository, and no second .slnx at this root.

  That finding is recorded in two other places, and all three say the same thing: docs/BUILD.md,
  which carries the build commands at length, and the repository root .editorconfig, whose MSBuild
  file section notes that no filter is authored for the format to match. If you are reading this
  because you were about to add one, all three are telling you not to.

  SCOPE: EXACTLY TWENTY-TWO PROJECTS, WHICH RECONCILES AS 6 PLUS 6 PLUS 4 PLUS 4 PLUS 2

      6   shared libraries under shared/
      6   sibling test projects, one per shared library
      4   service applications under services/
      4   service test projects, one per service
      2   the characterization capture driver and its test project, under characterization/tools/

  Every path below was verified to resolve against this working tree before being written. A path
  that does not resolve makes the whole solution unloadable, so the check is not optional. The
  twenty are also closed under project reference: following every reference of every project listed
  here reaches only projects that are themselves listed here, so this solution restores and builds
  its own complete graph with nothing arriving from outside it.

  ALL ELEVEN TEST PROJECTS ARE LOAD BEARING, AND SEVEN OF THEM ARE ONLY REACHABLE FROM HERE

  A test project omitted from a solution is not discovered by a test run against that solution. Its
  tests then do not execute, no coverage data is produced for the code it covers, and the result
  presents as a clean pass. That failure mode reads exactly like success, which makes it worse than
  a broken build.

  This matters most for the six shared test projects and for the capture driver's test project. The
  shared tree deliberately carries no solution of its own, and neither does characterization/tools,
  so THIS file is the only solution that enumerates those seven and therefore the only one from which
  they can be discovered and run. The four service test projects are additionally enumerated by their
  own per service solution, which is where the per service coverage gate is measured; their presence
  here is what lets a single root level test run cover the whole estate.

  SOLUTION FOLDERS ARE PRESENTATION ONLY

  The two `Folder` elements exist so the root of a twenty project solution is navigable in an IDE.
  They carry no build semantics of any kind: they are not compilation units, they do not group
  configurations, they do not express dependencies, and they impose no ordering. They intentionally
  mirror the two physical top level directories the refactor adds, shared/ and services/, so the
  logical view and the on disk layout cannot drift apart.

  ENTRY ORDER CARRIES NO BUILD SEMANTICS EITHER, AND THIS IS WORTH STATING EXPLICITLY

  MSBuild derives the build graph from project references, never from position in a solution. The
  ordering below is chosen purely for a human reader: folders in the alphabetical order the SDK
  emits, services in alphabetical order within their folder, and within each pair the subject
  project immediately before its test project so that a reader can confirm the ten pairings at a
  glance. Regenerating this file with the SDK would sort every entry strictly alphabetically and so
  place each test project before its subject. That is harmless, and the pairing below is the
  deliberate choice.

  This point is emphasised because the legacy system it replaces is the opposite case, and confusing
  the two would be a real defect rather than a stylistic slip. See the provenance note at the end.

  NOTHING IS CONFIGURED HERE, AND THERE IS NO VERSION

  No MSBuild property, no package version, no target framework, no custom configuration and no
  custom platform appears in this file. Only the standard Debug and Release configurations are in
  play, and nothing in the refactor requires any other, so none is declared. Everything else
  arrives from the repository root by MSBuild directory walk up:

      global.json                 pins the SDK version, roll forward confined to latest feature. The
                                  version itself is deliberately NOT repeated here - this line carried
                                  10.0.302 after the pin had moved on, which is the drift this whole
                                  comment block warns against, committed by the warning itself
      Directory.Build.props       net10.0, nullable, implicit usings, warnings as errors, and
                                  central package management turned on
      Directory.Packages.props    every package version, including the two mandatory security pins

  Restating any of that here would create a second source of truth able to drift a project off a
  mandatory pin, which is the exact failure central package management exists to prevent.

  No version number appears here either, and none may be added. The repository carries zero Git tags
  across its entire history, so no release is marked and no version can be inferred from it. The
  only version facts the repository asserts about itself belong to the legacy PowerBuilder runtime
  and say nothing about this tree.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT

  The four deferred capability areas of this refactor appear here in no form whatsoever: no project,
  no solution folder, no placeholder, no empty test project, and no commented out line reserving a
  slot for one. They are named and inventoried once, in docs/DEFERRED.md, and this file states their
  absence without restating their names precisely so that a text search of this file for any of them
  returns nothing at all, which makes the absence mechanically checkable rather than a matter of
  reading prose. They are deferred in full, and a solution folder labelled ready for a later phase
  would itself be a partial implementation of exactly what is deferred. Their only representation
  anywhere in this tree is four reserved routes in the Gateway application's own routing metadata,
  and nothing compiles behind those.

  The read only legacy PowerBuilder tree is absent because it is not a buildable unit. It is the
  behavioural oracle for parity testing and shares this one working directory with the .NET tree, so
  the exclusion is stated rather than left implicit: no compiled library, target or workspace file,
  no native binary, and nothing under ws_objects/, oldversion/, pack/, res/, samples/,
  sciter_control/ or the pre existing browser harness directories inside tests/ is referenced by any
  path below, and none ever may be.

  One directory the refactor adds is also absent, for the same reason of not being an MSBuild project:
  the end to end suite under tests/e2e is an npm and Playwright project, driven by its own package
  manifest.

  The characterization/ tree is PARTLY present, and the split is deliberate. Its recordings, workflow
  definitions, schema and capture plans are DATA and appear nowhere below. Its two projects under
  characterization/tools are ordinary MSBuild projects and are listed: the target side capture driver
  that turns a declared workflow into a normalized recording against a running service, and that
  driver's own test project. The driver is tooling and is never shipped in a container image, which is
  why it lives beside the data it drives rather than under services/ - and why it is marked not
  packable and takes no package reference of any kind.

  LEGACY PROVENANCE: ONE IDEA IS BORROWED, AND ONE MECHANISM IS DELIBERATELY NOT TRANSLATED

  The conceptual ancestor of this file is the PowerBuilder workspace pfw.pbw, which does no more
  than enumerate its child targets by relative path, index 0 pfw.pbt and index 1 pfwx.pbt, and name
  a default among them. The borrowed idea is exactly that: one container enumerating many buildable
  units, carrying no configuration of its own.

  What is NOT translated is the library list. The primary target pfw.pbt declares a single ordered
  list of 32 libraries, and its ORDER is load bearing there: PowerBuilder has no namespaces and no
  import statements, every global object lives in one flat namespace, and symbol resolution is
  decided by that ordering. There is no .slnx analogue and none is needed, because this tree uses
  explicit namespaces and explicit project references instead. No ordering semantics are encoded
  below, no build order directive appears, and none may be added.

  The two PowerBuilder build definitions are read as reference for build INTENT only, never
  translated, because neither is usable. ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/project.srj declares 29 library
  entries under COM:Appeon and includes pfw.utility.sqlite.pbl, while
  ws_objects/pfw.pbl.src/p_pfw.srj declares 28 under COM:Sybase, Inc., omits that library, and
  embeds developer workstation absolute paths under F:\pfw\ together with the retired library name
  pfwUtils.pbl. Both declare pfw.utility.imgcodec.pbl, which exists nowhere in the repository, so
  neither would build as written. This solution is therefore a clean creation, not a translation.

  RULES POSITION

  No user rules were provided for this project: the rules document contains exactly one line saying
  so. Nothing is invented or back filled from convention in their place. The binding constraints are
  the enterprise standard baseline together with the refactor's own named constraints, and every
  decision recorded above cites the concern that drives it.
-->
<Solution>

  <!--
    THE FOUR IN SCOPE SERVICES, EIGHT PROJECTS, PAIRED APPLICATION THEN TESTS.

    Each application project carries its own project references into shared/, so the shared closure
    arrives through those edges rather than through membership of this folder. No service references
    another service: they communicate only over the published contracts, across the network, which
    is what keeps them decomposed rather than collapsed. Ports and transports are documented in
    docs/ARCHITECTURE.md and are not expressed here, because a solution declares membership only.
  -->
  <Folder Name="/services/">

    <!--
      DataServices, port 5102 carrying REST and gRPC on one endpoint: the DataWindow retrieval,
      validation and update triple, the 22 event DataWindow chain, and the column expression engine.
    -->
    <Project Path="services/dataservices-service/PowerFramework.DataServices/PowerFramework.DataServices.csproj" />
    <Project Path="services/dataservices-service/PowerFramework.DataServices.Tests/PowerFramework.DataServices.Tests.csproj" />

    <!--
      Gateway, port 5105: the sole ingress and composition root, and the only place the four
      reserved routes for the deferred capability areas are declared as routing metadata.
    -->
    <Project Path="services/gateway-service/PowerFramework.Gateway/PowerFramework.Gateway.csproj" />
    <Project Path="services/gateway-service/PowerFramework.Gateway.Tests/PowerFramework.Gateway.Tests.csproj" />

    <!--
      Persistence, port 5101 carrying REST and gRPC on one endpoint: the only service that generates
      or executes SQL and the only one holding a storage provider.
    -->
    <Project Path="services/persistence-service/PowerFramework.Persistence/PowerFramework.Persistence.csproj" />
    <Project Path="services/persistence-service/PowerFramework.Persistence.Tests/PowerFramework.Persistence.Tests.csproj" />

    <!--
      Security, port 5104: the keyed cryptographic surface, and the sole token issuer in the
      system. Every other service holds verification material only.
    -->
    <Project Path="services/security-service/PowerFramework.Security/PowerFramework.Security.csproj" />
    <Project Path="services/security-service/PowerFramework.Security.Tests/PowerFramework.Security.Tests.csproj" />

  </Folder>

  <!--
    THE SIX SHARED LIBRARIES AND THEIR SIX TEST PROJECTS, TWELVE PROJECTS, PAIRED SUBJECT THEN TESTS.

    These carry pure behaviour and no input or output. PowerFramework.Contracts is the published
    boundary, the protocol and OpenAPI definitions plus their generated stubs, and it is the ONLY
    coupling permitted to cross a service boundary; it carries no behaviour of its own. The shared
    tree has no solution of its own, so this folder is the only place the six test projects can be
    discovered from.
  -->
  <Folder Name="/shared/">

    <!-- The published cross service boundary definition. -->
    <Project Path="shared/PowerFramework.Contracts/PowerFramework.Contracts.csproj" />
    <Project Path="shared/PowerFramework.Contracts.Tests/PowerFramework.Contracts.Tests.csproj" />

    <!-- Ordered map and vector containers, used by both DataServices and Persistence. -->
    <Project Path="shared/PowerFramework.Shared.Containers/PowerFramework.Shared.Containers.csproj" />
    <Project Path="shared/PowerFramework.Shared.Containers.Tests/PowerFramework.Shared.Containers.Tests.csproj" />

    <!-- Assertions, the assert payload protocol, stack traces and caller information. -->
    <Project Path="shared/PowerFramework.Shared.Diagnostics/PowerFramework.Shared.Diagnostics.csproj" />
    <Project Path="shared/PowerFramework.Shared.Diagnostics.Tests/PowerFramework.Shared.Diagnostics.Tests.csproj" />

    <!-- The event broker, its decomposed subscription topic identity and its tri valued veto. -->
    <Project Path="shared/PowerFramework.Shared.Eventful/PowerFramework.Shared.Eventful.csproj" />
    <Project Path="shared/PowerFramework.Shared.Eventful.Tests/PowerFramework.Shared.Eventful.Tests.csproj" />

    <!--
      The return code algebra with its tri state predicates, the constant catalogue, exceptions
      and the bit, word, ancestry and formatting primitives. The root of the shared graph.
    -->
    <Project Path="shared/PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel/PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel.csproj" />
    <Project Path="shared/PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel.Tests/PowerFramework.Shared.Kernel.Tests.csproj" />

    <!--
      Translation providers, categories and the silent passthrough fallback, reached from the
      DataServices validation path.
    -->
    <Project Path="shared/PowerFramework.Shared.Localization/PowerFramework.Shared.Localization.csproj" />
    <Project Path="shared/PowerFramework.Shared.Localization.Tests/PowerFramework.Shared.Localization.Tests.csproj" />

  </Folder>

  <!--
    THE CHARACTERIZATION TOOLING, TWO PROJECTS, PAIRED SUBJECT THEN TESTS.

    The target side capture driver and its tests. It is TOOLING, not a service: it ships in no
    container image, it is marked not packable, and it declares no package reference at all, so
    nothing arrives in the dependency graph through it. It is listed here for one reason above the
    others - characterization/tools carries no solution of its own, so this is the only solution from
    which its test project can be discovered, and a test project no solution enumerates presents as a
    clean pass while executing nothing.

    It references no service and no shared library. The driver speaks to a RUNNING service over its
    published HTTP contract, exactly as any other external client does, which is what keeps a capture
    an observation of the deployed system rather than of an in process object graph.
  -->
  <Folder Name="/characterization/">

    <Project Path="characterization/tools/PowerFramework.Characterization.Capture/PowerFramework.Characterization.Capture.csproj" />
    <Project Path="characterization/tools/PowerFramework.Characterization.Capture.Tests/PowerFramework.Characterization.Capture.Tests.csproj" />

  </Folder>

</Solution>
